			<div class="content" id="post-content-2335573">
									

<p>Could also be that they simply changed the name sometime along the way, or expanded it from a council to a full agency. They did have two major governmental restructurings between the first document and this one.</p>
<p>For me at least, the council brings to mind a small group of grey haired mystics and a steampunk-y scientist or two in charge of the ancient scrolls and vacuum-tube-sporting devices that tell you when that rock beast over in San'indō is about to rise again, and what you can do about it.</p>
<p>The Imperial Japanese Abnormal Matters Examination Agency brings to mind a modernized institution where you may still have the council involved somewhere, but with a more organized, bureaucratic structure like a staff, teams of agents, and a research department handling the day-to-day things. (Cue keystone kops style mental video of IJAMEA agents chasing after a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C5%8Dchin-obake">possessed table lamp</a> before one particularly enthusiastic agent takes it out with a tommy gun.)</p>

